<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malabar Coast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malabar Coast is the south-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and neighbouring areas, long famous for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and spice trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> on this coast in 1498, opening the sea route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to India. The coast became the first focus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s commercial and military efforts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malabar-coast.jpg" alt="Malabar Coast" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:user:Planemad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India_Malabar_Coast_locator_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
